# .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# A revision listed here is a change that touched many lines without changing
# what any of them MEAN. `git blame` normally attributes every line to whoever
# last touched it, so one whitespace sweep or one formatting pass makes the
# whole file look like it was written by whoever ran the tool -- and the person
# who actually wrote the logic disappears from the history.
#
# Listing such a revision here tells git to look THROUGH it and attribute each
# line to the last change that was not purely mechanical.
#
# This file changes nothing about the repository. It is a hint, and it only
# takes effect on a machine configured to read it (see README, "Configure git
# before your first commit") or in CI, where the flag is passed explicitly.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One full 40-character commit SHA per line. Blank lines and lines starting
# with '#' are ignored. No commas, no quotes, no trailing explanation -- the
# parser takes the whole line as a revision, so a comment on the same line
# would be parsed as part of it.
#
#   e5f6a7b8c9d0e1f2a3b4c5d6e7f8a9b0c1d2e3f4
#
# Get the SHA of a commit you want to skip:
#
#   git log --format='%H %s' -- <path>     # find it
#   git log -1 --format=%H                 # or copy the tip
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO ADD A REVISION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Make the mechanical change as its OWN commit, on its own.
#    A formatting commit must contain nothing but formatting. If it also
#    fixes a bug, the bug fix is now invisible and cannot be skipped.
#
# 2. Verify the commit changed no behaviour. For a large sweep that is not
#    obvious by inspection, compare the compiled output or the test results
#    before and after. A revision that is wrongly listed here does not bury a
#    fix -- it only makes blame point at the wrong commit, which is a smaller
#    problem than it sounds but still a real one.
#
# 3. Append its full SHA below, under the appropriate heading.
#
# 4. Commit this file IN THE SAME COMMIT as nothing else. The list is a
#    convenience for readers; an update buried inside another change will not
#    be noticed when it is wrong.
#
# 5. Anyone who has already cloned gets the update on pull. Nobody needs to
#    re-run any command -- `blame.ignoreRevsFile` is read per invocation.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHEN NOT TO USE THIS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only for changes that are provably non-semantic. Never list:
#
#   - a commit that renamed a type or a file (that IS information)
#   - a commit that reordered members for readability but also changed one
#   - a commit that "mostly" formatted
#
# If you are unsure whether a commit is purely mechanical, do not list it. The
# history being slightly noisy is a much smaller cost than a fix that has been
# hidden from the person reading the file six months later.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
